  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nWet pattern expected over the next 48 hours. Broad southwest\nflow ahead of an approaching upper level shortwave trough that\nreach the region for the weekend. First round of shower activity\nthis afternoon will push out the region by this afternoon with\ncloudy skies and continued low level moisture fetch remaining in\nits wake. Stationary boundary will develop just off the Maine\ncoast tomorrow that will be a focus area for steady rain.\nStill enough spread in the guidance to support the potential\nthat the outer islands and necks could be on the edge of the\nheavier rain axis tomorrow afternoon into Friday night. Trended\nup the rainfall amounts a bit for the first wave tomorrow closer\nto the main guidance as enough guidance has trended just\nslightly westward again. A brief break in the rain is possible\nSaturday morning before the upper level approaches and captures\nsome of the coastal moisture fetch. Its even possible a surface\nlow pressure system will develop in the Gulf of Maine,\nincreasing low level dynamics for a developing deformation zone\nacross the region Saturday night. This will be the prime window\nfor rainfall chances across the interior through midday Sunday\nas the developing surface and upper level features move eastward\nby Sunday afternoon ending the widespread rainfall. Overall\nexpecting close to 2\" for Penobscot Bay Islands with only a\nquarter of an inch for the CT River Valley. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nAs the consolidated low moves northeast, another wave in tow \nwill dive out of Ontario/Quebec through New England Sunday night\ninto Monday. This will help bring cooler temperatures back to \nthe region as well as additional light precipitation. Daytime \nhighs look to top out in the 40s across the mountains with 50s \ntowards the interior and coast for much of the week.\n\nWhile downstream precipitation could be light to none with this\nwave, it may be an effective upslope precipitation setup for \nthe western Whites. Combined with cooler temps, its possible for\nsome light snow or snow showers to occur for the Whites and \nUS/CAN border mountains. Will need to monitor how far north or \nsouth the core of moisture passes through the Northeast given \nsome variability in guidance at this time. This will determine \nif accumulations are expected at elevation, as well as if any of\nthe rain showers will feature measurable amounts from the \nfoothills to the coast.\n\nThen, the pattern through midweek supports additional chances \nfor light precipitation, arriving on northwest flow. With\nlimitations on moisture source, measurable precipitation outside\nof the mountains appears limited, with upslope precip\npotentially continuing on and off through Wednesday or Thursday.",
